      "text": "The most common reason for this is **unwanted whitespace** between your subfigures. In LaTeX, a \"new line\" in your code often counts as a single space character. If you have two images that take up `0.48\\textwidth` each, plus one \"space\" character between them, the total width can exceed `1.0\\textwidth`, pushing the second image off the page.\n\nHere is the corrected code using **comment characters (`%`)** to remove those hidden spaces and ensuring the widths are safe.\n\n### The Corrected Code\n```latex\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n    \\centering\n    % --- Top Row ---\n    % Use exactly 0.49\\textwidth and put a % at the end of the line\n    \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n        \\centering\n        \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{image_a.png}\n        \\caption{Image A}\n    \\end{subfigure}% \u003c--- This % is CRITICAL to remove the space\n    \\hfill\n    \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n        \\centering\n        \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{image_b.png}\n        \\caption{Image B}\n    \\end{subfigure}\n\n    \\vspace{0.5cm} % Vertical space between rows\n\n    % --- Bottom Row ---\n    \\begin{subfigure}[b]{1.0\\textwidth}\n        \\centering\n        \\includegraphics[width\u003d0.8\\textwidth]{image_c.png} % Image C centered at 80% width\n        \\caption{Image C}\n    \\end{subfigure}\n\n    \\caption{Main figure caption.}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{document}\n```\n\n### Why this fixes the \"Off the Edge\" issue:\n\n1.  **The Percent Sign (`%`)**: Notice the `%` immediately after `\\end{subfigure}` for image A. This tells LaTeX: \"Do not treat the next line break as a space.\" Without this, LaTeX adds a tiny bit of width which pushes image B over the margin.\n2.  **`\\hfill` vs Space**: `\\hfill` is a \"rubber\" space that expands to fill whatever is left. By removing the literal space with `%`, `\\hfill` can do its job perfectly.\n3.  **Check your Document Margins**: If you are using a two-column document (like a journal template), use `\\begin{figure*}` instead of `\\begin{figure}`. A standard `figure` environment in a two-column paper will try to squish everything into one narrow column, causing images to bleed off the side.\n4.  **The `width` parameter**: \n    *   Inside the `subfigure`, `width\u003d\\textwidth` refers to the width of the **subfigure container** (0.48 of the page). \n    *   If you set the subfigure container to `1.0\\textwidth` (the full row) but the image is still too wide, reduce the `\\includegraphics` width (e.g., `width\u003d0.8\\textwidth`) to keep it centered and safe.\n\n### Quick Fix for \"Overwide\" Images\nIf your image is naturally very wide and refuses to stay in the margins, you can \"force\" it to center itself relative to the text block using this command inside your subfigure:\n```latex\n\\makebox[\\textwidth][c]{\\includegraphics[width\u003d1.1\\textwidth]{image_c.png}}\n```\n*(This allows the image to bleed slightly into the left and right margins equally rather than just falling off the right side.)*",
