      "parts": [{
        "text": "The most common reason for this is **unwanted whitespace** between your subfigures. In LaTeX, a \"new line\" in your code often counts as a single space character. If you have two images"
      }, {
        "text": " that take up `0.48\\textwidth` each, plus one \"space\" character between them, the total"
      }, {
        "text": " width can exceed `1.0\\textwidth`, pushing the second image off the page.\n\nHere is the corrected code"
      }, {
        "text": " using **comment characters (`%`)** to remove those hidden spaces and ensuring the widths are safe.\n\n### The Corrected Code\n"
      }, {
        "text": "```latex\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n    \\"
      }, {
        "text": "centering\n    % --- Top Row ---\n    % Use exactly 0.49\\textwidth and put a % at the end"
      }, {
        "text": " of the line\n    \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n        \\centering\n        \\includegraphics"
      }, {
        "text": "[width\u003d\\textwidth]{image_a.png}\n        \\caption{Image A}\n    \\end{"
      }, {
        "text": "subfigure}% \u003c--- This % is CRITICAL to remove the space\n    \\hfill\n    \\begin{subfigure}[b]{"
      }, {
        "text": "0.48\\textwidth}\n        \\centering\n        \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{image_b."
      }, {
        "text": "png}\n        \\caption{Image B}\n    \\end{subfigure}\n\n    \\vspace{0."
      }, {
        "text": "5cm} % Vertical space between rows\n\n    % --- Bottom Row ---\n    \\begin{subfigure}[b]{1"
      }, {
        "text": ".0\\textwidth}\n        \\centering\n        \\includegraphics[width\u003d0.8\\textwidth]{image_c.png"
      }, {
        "text": "} % Image C centered at 80% width\n        \\caption{Image C}\n    \\end{subfigure}"
      }, {
        "text": "\n\n    \\caption{Main figure caption.}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{document}\n```\n\n### Why this"
      }, {
        "text": " fixes the \"Off the Edge\" issue:\n\n1.  **The Percent Sign (`%`)**: Notice the `%` immediately"
      }, {
        "text": " after `\\end{subfigure}` for image A. This tells LaTeX: \"Do not treat the next line break as a space.\""
      }, {
        "text": " Without this, LaTeX adds a tiny bit of width which pushes image B over the margin.\n2.  **`\\"
      }, {
        "text": "hfill` vs Space**: `\\hfill` is a \"rubber\" space that expands to fill whatever is left. By removing"
      }, {
        "text": " the literal space with `%`, `\\hfill` can do its job perfectly.\n3.  **Check your Document Marg"
      }, {
        "text": "ins**: If you are using a two-column document (like a journal template), use `\\begin{figure*}"
      }, {
        "text": "` instead of `\\begin{figure}`. A standard `figure` environment in a two-column paper will try to squ"
      }, {
        "text": "ish everything into one narrow column, causing images to bleed off the side.\n4.  **The `width` parameter"
      }, {
        "text": "**: \n    *   Inside the `subfigure`, `width\u003d\\textwidth` refers to the width of the **subfigure"
      }, {
        "text": " container** (0.48 of the page). \n    *   If you set the subfigure container to `1"
      }, {
        "text": ".0\\textwidth` (the full row) but the image is still too wide, reduce the `\\includegraphics` width"
      }, {
        "text": " (e.g., `width\u003d0.8\\textwidth`) to keep it centered and safe.\n\n### Quick Fix"
      }, {
        "text": " for \"Overwide\" Images\nIf your image is naturally very wide and refuses to stay in the margins, you can \""
      }, {
        "text": "force\" it to center itself relative to the text block using this command inside your subfigure:\n```latex\n\\makebox[\\"
      }, {
        "text": "textwidth][c]{\\includegraphics[width\u003d1.1\\textwidth]{image_c.png}}\n```\n*("
      }, {
        "text": "This allows the image to bleed slightly into the left and right margins equally rather than just falling off the right side.)*"
